diff --git a/docs/inventory-screen.md b/docs/inventory-screen.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5f8b49b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/inventory-screen.md
@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+# Inventory screen
+
+The screen where players see every block type, how many of each they have, which ones are
+locked and at what level, and pick the block to build with.
+
+Code: `src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UI/` (`InventoryUI` mounts it from
+`Main.client.luau`). Stories for UI Labs: `src/ServerScriptService/Stories/Inventory/`.
+
+## Player and moment
+
+- **Who:** mostly children (assume ages 6 to 12), mostly on phones held sideways. The game also
+  runs on PC and Xbox.
+- **When:** in the middle of building. The player wants to switch blocks, or to check what they
+  have, and then get back to building. That is why the screen opens in one press, picking takes
+  one press, and the HUD button shows the current block so players don't have to open the screen
+  just to check it.
+
+## What's on screen
+
+| Element | Job | How often it is needed | Where |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Blocks button (HUD) | Opens the screen; shows the current block's color and count | Always | Top right, below the Roblox top bar, clear of the thumbstick and jump button corners |
+| Panel title row | "Blocks", the player's level, close button (X) | While open | Top of the panel; X is always in the top-right corner |
+| Grid of 10 tiles | Every block type, how many the player has, locked or picked | While open | 5 by 2 grid, no scrolling at any screen size |
+| Detail strip | Explains one block in words: name, count, what to do or why it's locked | While open | Bottom of the panel |
+| Input hints | "1-0 Pick, B Close" on keyboard; A/B glyphs on gamepad | While open, not on touch | Right side of the detail strip |
+
+Tile states and their cues. None of them depend on color alone:
+
+| State | Cues |
+|---|---|
+| Normal | Color swatch, name, "x48" count |
+| Picked | Thick yellow outline, "Picked" tag, detail strip says "You are building with this." |
+| Locked | Darkened swatch, padlock, "Lv 4" tag instead of the count, gray name, detail strip says "Unlocks at level 4." |
+| None owned | "x0" in gray; detail strip says "You have none. Collect some!" The block can still be picked |
+| Hover / pressed | Lighter background on hover; the tile sinks 3 units and darkens when pressed |
+| Gamepad focus | Roblox's selection outline; the focused tile shows in the detail strip |
+
+## Opening and closing
+
+| | Touch | Mouse and keyboard | Controller |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Open | Tap the Blocks button | Click the Blocks button, or press **B** | Press **Y** |
+| Close | Tap **X**, or tap anywhere outside the panel | Click **X**, click outside the panel, or press **B** again | Press **B** (or Y again) |
+
+- The screen opens with a 0.25 s slide up and a backdrop fade. It closes instantly. With
+  Roblox's Reduced Motion setting on, it doesn't slide, and locked tiles don't shake.
+- While it is open, Roblox's touch controls (thumbstick and jump button) are hidden and block
+  placing is turned off, so a tap outside the panel only closes it and never places a block.
+- The backdrop and panel follow Roblox's Background Transparency setting.
+- Esc stays with Roblox's own menu. That's why the panel has its own close key (B) and an X
+  button that touch, mouse, and gamepad focus can all reach.
+
+## Actions on each input
+
+| Action | Touch | Mouse and keyboard | Controller |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Pick a block | Tap its tile | Click its tile, or press its number key (1 to 9, then 0), with the screen open or closed | Move focus with the stick or D-pad, press **A** |
+| See details of a block | Tap it (the detail strip shows the last tapped block) | Hover it, or click it | Move focus to it |
+| See why a block is locked | Tap it: the tile shakes, and the detail strip says what level unlocks it | Same, or hover it | Focus it, or press A on it |
+| See counts and locks | Shown on every tile | Same | Same |
+
+- A locked block can't be picked, from the screen or with its number key.
+- Picking is one press and the screen stays open, so players can see what they picked. Closing
+  takes one more press. Picking can be undone by picking again, so there is no confirmation.
+- On a controller, focus starts on the picked tile when the screen opens. It moves there too if
+  the player picks up a controller while the screen is open. Focus can't leave the panel, and
+  closing gives focus back to whatever had it before.
+- Number-key badges (1 to 0) show on tiles only when keyboard and mouse is the main input.
+
+## Sizes
+
+- Touch targets: tiles are 98 by 86 units on phones and 120 by 140 elsewhere, the close button is
+  44 or 56, and the HUD button is 88 by 92. They are all above the 44-unit floor.
+- On a phone (short side under 600 units) the panel uses a compact layout that fits a 667 by 375
+  screen at nearly full size. Anything smaller scales the panel down, never below 0.6.
+- Larger screens scale the whole UI from 1080p, with 1.25 times extra on TVs, so detail text is
+  about 27 px at 1080p on Xbox.
+
+## Open questions for playtests
+
+These are design defaults, not proven choices. Test them with about five new players on phones:
+
+- Should picking a block close the screen? Today it stays open; watch whether kids look for a way
+  back to building.
+- Should blocks the player has none of be pickable? Today they are, with a "Collect some!" note.
+- Is the Blocks button found without help? Is **B** / **Y** discovered without the hint?
+- On Xbox, does our **B** close the screen before Roblox's own B leaves UI navigation? Check
+  in the Controller Emulator.
+- Does the layout hold at Roblox's Largest text size? Check that the detail strip's status line
+  doesn't overflow on phones.
+
+Analytics to log: screen opened (with input type), block picked (block, from screen or number
+key), locked tile tapped (block, player level), and time from open to close.
